  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer with increasing clouds. Rain chances\nreturn late today into tonight for CT, RI and southeast MA.\n\nLatest guidance continued to indicated a weak low pressure \nmoving close to the region, emphasizing rainfall chances\ngenerally south of I-90 tonight. It's worth noting, though, \nthat showers do remain possible across all of southern New \nEngland starting in the late evening hours. There's a low chance \nfor some embedded thunder, but that is generally relegated to \nthe south coast and the southern waters. Not anticipating a \nwashout. Higher confidence that showery conditions continue \novernight Sunday towards the south coast, clearing out heading \ninto Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Backdoor cold front Monday brings onshore flow,\ndecreased cloud cover and lower temperatures.\n\nStill some question as to how quickly a backdoor front will make\nit across southern New England late tonight into Monday. Latest\nguidance suite continued to favor a faster timing, leaving\nsouthern New England on the drier, albeit cooler, side. May not\nbe quite as much of a temperature difference well away from\neastern MA\n\nCloud cover then increases Monday night with rainfall chances\nincreasing from west to east, especially after midnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled Tuesday through Thursday with several\nopportunities for showers and thunderstorms, with a chance for \nsome to become strong in western MA and CT. Timing which days \nmay have better storm chances is still uncertain. \n\nTuesday through Thursday looks more active/unsettled, as the \nbackdoor frontal boundary stalls from SW to NE through NY State \nand the northeast fringe of a substantial heat ridge centered \nover the Ohio Valley by Tuesday tries to build into the \nnorthern Mid Atlantic and perhaps into a small portion of \nsouthern New England. This places Southern New England between \na more stable and seasonable airmass to the northeast and a \nwarm/hot, humid and conditionally unstable airmass to our \nsouthwest.\n\nAbove this stalled surface boundary lies a belt of strong NW \nflow aloft (e.g. 300 mb winds approaching 100 kt), which tends \nto favor shortwave energy impulses moving SE along the stalled \nfront. Several opportunities for showers and thunderstorms seem \nlikely during this period, but the details of which are \nuncertain. Those hinge on the exact placement/evolution of the \nfrontal boundary, if we can tap into the more unstable air to \nour southwest, and the timing of shortwaves in the strong NW \nflow aloft.\n\nMachine-learning guidance from the GEFS and Colorado State show\nsome severe weather potential for our western/southwest counties\nin this period, and SPC highlights a small sliver of western \nHampden County and western/central Hartford County in a 15% \nprobability for severe weather for Wednesday, uncommon for what \nis a Day-5 forecast. Am skeptical we get entrenched into the \nmore unstable air to our southwest but the best potential for \ndoing so would more likely be south and west of Worcester. \nSomething that we'll need to monitor as we get into the \nmesoscale model range.\n\nGaining confidence in a period of showers late Monday night into\nTuesday evening. There could be a brief break late Tuesday\nnight into early Wednesday. Another round of showers is possible\nfrom Wednesday afternoon into Thursday. Will have to see if this\ntiming persists with later forecasts, but this is the best\nestimate at this time.",
